From 21bc01d394012ad0e4e9f899a95bf5a466cfb097 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ilan Joselevich <personal@ilanjoselevich.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 06:34:31 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 13/15] workers: report the host's CPU and memory, and show
 them to watchers

Slot use says how full a builder's slots are, not how busy its machine
is. A pool whose slots look 60 to 90% full while its CPUs sit at 30%
looks healthy, and nothing on the stream says otherwise.

Builders read /proc/stat and /proc/meminfo once a second. Busy CPUs are
the share of jiffies since the last read that were neither idle nor
iowait, times the online CPUs, in thousandths of a CPU. Memory in use is
MemTotal minus MemAvailable. Both are host-wide, so any other process on
the host counts too. WorkerReport carries them in a session's first
report, then when busy moved by at least 5% of the CPUs, memory in use
by 5% of the total, or 30 s passed since they last went out. Like the
rest of the report, they go only to a scheduler that asked with
ReportsWanted. Hosts without /proc, such as macOS, send none.

The scheduler keeps a builder's last values, with busy capped at its
CPUs and memory in use at its total. WorkerState gains
cpu_busy_millicores, cpu_cores, mem_used_bytes and mem_total_bytes. They
go out with the report-driven WorkerStates, at most once a second per
builder. Hello clears them until the session's first report, so a
builder that sends none shows none.

substituter-tally-test covers the parsing, the sampler, the real /proc
files and when reports carry the load. events-test covers what watchers
get.
